From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jay Leaton <58495321+jayleaton@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 11:47:55 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 71/72] server: a whole tool call to an undeclared function
 name goes out as a tool call, as vLLM returns it

A well-formed call to a tool the request did not declare stayed in the reply's text, so the reply ended with
finish "stop" and no tool_calls. OpenCode 2 declares its shell tool as `shell`; DeepSeek-V4.1 sometimes calls
`bash` after a history of shell calls, and the client took the DSML block as the final answer and ended the turn
(4 of 30 trials in an outside OpenCode 2.2.7 benchmark). On vLLM the call goes back as a tool call, the client
answers "unknown tool" and the model retries.

be8bccbec made such calls go out under their own name, as docs/api.md still says; 7be90a36b turned that back into
text the next day to match the frozen Python goldens. tool_parse.callName again returns an undeclared name when it
is 1 to 64 of [A-Za-z0-9_-] and the arguments are a finite JSON object, for every whole envelope (DSML, XML, JSON,
GLM, Gemma). Unchanged: a name with spaces, dots or other characters, non-finite arguments, malformed or unclosed
blocks, bare JSON naming no offered tool (a structured answer), requests with no tools, calls in the reasoning,
closeCall (an end token inside a call closes it only for a declared tool), calls max_tokens cut, and LFM2's call
lists.

A stream sends such a call as the final parse returns it. DSML invokes stream as written on this line, so an
undeclared function name now streams its header as written; a block holding an offered invoke and an undeclared
one sends each once, where the whole-block parse would have sent the offered call a second time.

Tests: parser cases for each format in both call modes; the cases that stay text; closeCall; an HTTP test over
whole and streamed replies, one-call and parallel, including DeepSeek-V4.1's own DSML reply captured on the
Sparks; streamer tests for an offered call followed by an undeclared one, as XML envelopes and inside one DSML
block. The new tests fail on 8e744bf70. The retained-envelope tests keep their checks with names that are no
function names, and test-golden lists the four bad_tool goldens as known differences.
